---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { STORIES } from '../../engine/chapters';
---
<Base
  title="Sysadmin Quests"
  lang="en"
  other={{ href: '/fr/', lang: 'fr', name: 'Français' }}
  description="Learn Linux system administration by playing, in your browser, with real commands: moving around, searching, permissions, logs, networking, git, backups."
>
  <h1>Sysadmin Quests</h1>
  <p class="intro">
    Adventures where you act with <strong>real Linux commands</strong>. Every place is a
    directory, every object a file. {STORIES.length} independent stories: start with The Eraser
    if you have never opened a terminal.
  </p>
  <p class="intro">
    Stuck? Do as the pros do: <code>man command</code> opens the manual,
    <code>command --help</code> gives a summary, and <code>man -k word</code> finds the command
    that does what you want.
  </p>
  <p class="intro">
    The game was written in French and translated into English. The places and the documents
    you read have English names (<code>cd Lighthouse</code>, <code>cat email_kofi.txt</code>);
    some things on the stories' servers (folders, websites, databases) keep their French
    names, as they would on a server run by a French team.
  </p>
  <noscript><p class="avertissement">This game needs JavaScript to simulate a terminal in your browser.</p></noscript>

  <quetes-de-sysadmin lang="en"></quetes-de-sysadmin>

  <section aria-labelledby="embed">
    <h2 id="embed">Put the game on your site</h2>
    <p>The game is free software and fits in any page with two lines:</p>
    <pre><code>{`<script type="module" src="https://sysadmin.rouages.net/integration/quetes-de-sysadmin.js"></script>
<quetes-de-sysadmin lang="en"></quetes-de-sysadmin>`}</code></pre>
    <p>Full instructions: <a href="https://github.com/Rouages-du-net/quetes-de-sysadmin/blob/main/docs/EMBEDDING.md">docs/EMBEDDING.md</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="names">
    <h2 id="names">Why names ending in .internal and .example?</h2>
    <p>
      The machines in the game are fictional, but their names follow the real rules: <code>.internal</code>
      is reserved for private networks (ICANN, 2024), <code>.example</code> for examples
      (<a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6761">RFC 6761</a>), and the addresses 192.0.2.x, 198.51.100.x
      and 203.0.113.x for documentation (<a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc5737">RFC 5737</a>). A
      made-up name could belong to someone: what you try here never targets a real machine.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="teach">
    <h2 id="teach">For teachers</h2>
    <p>Goal, prerequisites, new commands and time for each chapter: <a href="/en/teachers/">the quest sheets</a>.</p>
  </section>
</Base>

<script>
  import '../../element';
</script>
'